A renderable is a **value**, so it reaches every position a value reaches: a
spec's own object, an array item, and a field whose value is itself an object.
The value travels as JSON when it is nested, and the field is resolved in place —
the fragment nests the way a bound `mdp` value does:

@[Card](/docs)@@anchorA{specs: {caption: ;;*nested* in an object;;}, tokens: [;;one;;, "two"]}

Nesting a field inside a field needs escapes, because a bare `;;` closes the
outer span (`\;` is a semicolon). The inner element's head is read when the
payload runs, so its field is a field again — the escaped semicolons stay as
text, which is the price of one escape level and the reason a deep tree is
better bound than written:

@[Card](/docs)@@anchorA{note: ;;outer \;\;@[In](/u)@@anchorB{v: \;\;inner *i*\;\;} \;\; tail;;}

A `bind` value and a renderable are the same thing written two ways — one names
a payload the data block authored, the other writes it at the position:

{{{mdp[caption] A **bound** payload}}}

@[Card](/docs)@@anchorA{note: $caption, body: ;;A **written** payload;;}

A position that needs **text** is not reserved (§6.7, D-RF-6): the image wants a
string for `alt`, so it reads the authored `;;…;;` spelling and behaves exactly
as it did before renderables existed:

![;;*alt* payload;;](https://example.com/chart.png)
